{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 70,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum mechanics is incredibly successful at describing the small scales of  reality, but it's usually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5000,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, there are some fringe cases where\nits distinct features manifest on scales we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9540,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can observe—in things like superfluids,\nor the interiors of collapsed stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15170,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s also possible that our entire galaxy\nis filled with a reverberating quantum mechanical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20260,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wave that literally holds the galaxy together—and\nin fact explains all the dark matter that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25830,
    "dDurationMs": 1099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we see across the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26929,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this isn’t even a fringe theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27429,
    "dDurationMs": 4347,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s axionic dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37126,
    "dDurationMs": 4574,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Around 80% of the mass of the universe seems\nto be this completely invisible stuff we call"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41700,
    "dDurationMs": 1340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Several lines of evidence tell us that there\nmust be an unseen gravitational influence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47520,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48520,
    "dDurationMs": 4839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, it’s needed to hold galaxies\ntogether and even to allow galaxies to form"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53359,
    "dDurationMs": 1130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54489,
    "dDurationMs": 4381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although we can’t directly detect dark matter,\nwe can see how it behaves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58870,
    "dDurationMs": 5079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It acts like a bunch of low-temperature particles\nthat barely interact with anything except"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63949,
    "dDurationMs": 1481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "via gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65430,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This “cold dark matter” model is the CDM\nin our Lambda-CDM model of cosmology, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71260,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s been pretty successful in explaining\nhow the tiny density fluctuations in the cosmic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76880,
    "dDurationMs": 4849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "microwave background collapsed under gravity\nto form the types of structures we see in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81729,
    "dDurationMs": 1070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82799,
    "dDurationMs": 5261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The mainstream hypothesis is that cold dark\nmatter is some kind of exotic particle—something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88060,
    "dDurationMs": 6230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outside the standard model of particle physics\nand not yet detected in our particle experiments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94290,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the main-mainstream version of such a\nparticle is called the WIMP - which stands"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98920,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for weakly interacting massive particle - and\nthere are many speculative candidates for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103950,
    "dDurationMs": 4943,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the type of particle that could behave in\nthe way needed for the CDM model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108893,
    "dDurationMs": 5087,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now, all you need to know about WIMPs is that they can form gravitationally bound structures,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113980,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "known as dark matter “halos”, through\na process called virialization."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120520,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine a spherical “cloud” of WIMPs collapsing\nunder its own gravity, as might have existed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125850,
    "dDurationMs": 2049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the very early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127899,
    "dDurationMs": 4611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The particles will fall towards the center\nof mass, pass through it due to their weakly-interacting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132510,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nature, and come out the other side on chaotic\norbits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135430,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’ll end up flying around the halo with\nrandom orbits which give them an effective"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140340,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“pressure” that keeps the halo “puffy”\nand supported against further gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145160,
    "dDurationMs": 1210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146370,
    "dDurationMs": 5229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark matter halos can be formed in this way\nin many different sizes, ranging from a small"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151599,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fraction of a galaxy to entire clusters of\ngalaxies; and it’s within these halos that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156989,
    "dDurationMs": 2641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies, themselves, can form."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159630,
    "dDurationMs": 6710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As I mentioned, in cosmological simulations\nthe CDM model does a pretty nice job of reproducing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166340,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the structure of the universe we see today,\non scales all the way from individual galaxies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171190,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to giant galaxy clusters and the filaments\nof the cosmic web."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174880,
    "dDurationMs": 4389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we have reasons to be concerned about\nthe WIMP model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179269,
    "dDurationMs": 4131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing, despite decades of effort we’ve\nnever been able to detect a WIMP particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183400,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That doesn’t rule anything out - for example,\nWIMPs may be too massive to be created by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187760,
    "dDurationMs": 1750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "current particle colliders."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189510,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s another reason to wonder if\nWIMPs are really the answer - and that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193900,
    "dDurationMs": 5309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because the standard cold dark matter model\ndoesn’t quite do a perfect job of explaining"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199209,
    "dDurationMs": 1090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structure formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200299,
    "dDurationMs": 6141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It appears to miss some of the smaller scale details, and I’ll come back to exactly how"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206440,
    "dDurationMs": 1070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it fails."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207510,
    "dDurationMs": 3649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately, there’s another compelling\ncandidate for dark matter that could solve"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211159,
    "dDurationMs": 1771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some of these problems."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212930,
    "dDurationMs": 2449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The candidate particle is the axion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215379,
    "dDurationMs": 5041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve talked previously about this ultra-light\nhypothetical particle, but we haven’t talked"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220420,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about how the axion could explain dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223170,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we should, because it’s exceptionally\nweird, and involves the quantum nature of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227660,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these particles becoming manifest on galactic\nscales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230870,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All particles are really wave-like on the\nsmallest scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234420,
    "dDurationMs": 4071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum field theory describes every particle\nas a localized vibration in its own quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238491,
    "dDurationMs": 1108,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239599,
    "dDurationMs": 5181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The size-scale of that vibration is defined\nby the de Broglie wavelength, which gets smaller"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244780,
    "dDurationMs": 2390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as the momentum of the particle increases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247170,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But momentum depends on mass, and so massive\nparticles tend to have very short de Broglie"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253060,
    "dDurationMs": 1210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavelengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254270,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means we don’t observe their wave-like\nnature on human scales - there they just act"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258930,
    "dDurationMs": 1119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260049,
    "dDurationMs": 4361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s how the massive WIMP particles are\nsupposed to behave."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264410,
    "dDurationMs": 4479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the lower the mass of the particle, the\nlonger its de Broglie wavelength."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268889,
    "dDurationMs": 5861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In principle, that wavelength could reach\nhuman, or even astronomical scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274750,
    "dDurationMs": 1470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What would that even look like?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276220,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s try to imagine it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278220,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To start with, we’ll think about WIMP-like\ndark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282340,
    "dDurationMs": 2090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How close together would those particles be?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284430,
    "dDurationMs": 6601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say the mass of each dark matter particle\nis around 1 GeV — roughly the mass of a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291031,
    "dDurationMs": 4989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proton — we would need about 1 of these\nparticles per cubic meter on average in order"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296020,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to produce the amount of excess gravity not\nexplained by visible matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300160,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there would be WIMPs in the room with you right now, but those WIMPs are really far"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305050,
    "dDurationMs": 5179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "apart from each other compared to their own\ntiny de Broglie wavelengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310229,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If they also only interact by short-range\nforces then WIMPs basically never bump into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315169,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316169,
    "dDurationMs": 7171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s what we need for a dark matter particle—something\nthat very rarely interacts even with itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323340,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, imagine that we could tune the mass of\nthe dark matter particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327270,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The lower the mass, the more dark matter particles\nwe need to produce the gravity that we observe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332140,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333340,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The particles end up closer together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335610,
    "dDurationMs": 3899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But at the same time, their de Broglie wavelengths\nincrease."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339509,
    "dDurationMs": 6231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once the mass drops below a meV or so - around\na millionth that of an electron - we start"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345740,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to run into some interesting effects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347800,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If these particles are slow moving, as expected\nfor cold dark matter, then their de Broglie"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353340,
    "dDurationMs": 3389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavelengths are around the same as their separation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356729,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The quantum wavefunctions of the particles\nstart to overlap."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359949,
    "dDurationMs": 3661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we’re in the realm of quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363610,
    "dDurationMs": 4839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As we’ve seen in recent episodes, the behavior\nof overlapping quantum particles depends on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368449,
    "dDurationMs": 1271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their type."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369720,
    "dDurationMs": 4379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fermions will repel each other - which would\nrule them out as weakly-interacting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374099,
    "dDurationMs": 7281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But boson wavefunctions can comfortably overlap,\nso the particles can still ignore each other,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381380,
    "dDurationMs": 1610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just like dark matter is supposed to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382990,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s keep going."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383990,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As we reduce the mass of these bosons even\nfurther, the overlapping vibrations will blend"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388830,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "together into one seamless, coherently oscillating\nfield."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392860,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Think about ripples in a pool that have reflected\noff the edges until you can’t see the original"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397890,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sources and the surface is a pattern of resonant\noscillations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401770,
    "dDurationMs": 8869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At a certain low mass - around one thousandth of an electron volt - any trace of individual dark matter particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410639,
    "dDurationMs": 6501,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will have vanished and we’re left with a\ndark matter field that behaves as a pure wave."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417140,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, if you’ve been keeping up with recent Space Time episodes, you might recall what happens"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421160,
    "dDurationMs": 4479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when you have a fluid of non-interacting bosons\nwith overlapping wavefunctions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425639,
    "dDurationMs": 5791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You get a Bose-Einstein condensate, manifesting\nas a superfluid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431430,
    "dDurationMs": 4041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because the particles of a superfluid can’t\ninteract, they can’t exchange energy, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435471,
    "dDurationMs": 1549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means they have no friction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437020,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They flow perfectly, with zero viscosity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440280,
    "dDurationMs": 4099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s what axionic dark matter would\nbe—a superfluid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444379,
    "dDurationMs": 7130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, if the axion predicted by quantum\nchromodynamics has a mass of around 10^-5"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451509,
    "dDurationMs": 5972,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eV, it would have formed in sufficient numbers\nin the early universe to account for all of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457481,
    "dDurationMs": 3479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the dark matter and to exist in this superfluid\nstate.."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460960,
    "dDurationMs": 4579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if dark matter is a superfluid, how does\nit actually behave?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465539,
    "dDurationMs": 4331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that modeling dark matter as regular\nparticles works pretty well."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469870,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This new type of dark matter would need to\ndo at least as good a job as WIMPs for us"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474270,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to consider them as a valid alternative."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477880,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this type of dark matter would behave\nvery differently to a WIMP."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481960,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the picture I painted earlier\nof WIMPs falling towards the center and ending"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486240,
    "dDurationMs": 4239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up in random orbits doesn’t work for the\naxion superfluid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490479,
    "dDurationMs": 5881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead, imagine some clouds of axionic dark\nmatter in the early universe as simple spherical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496360,
    "dDurationMs": 1573,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves like ripples."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497933,
    "dDurationMs": 4947,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These would fall towards each other under\nthe influence of gravity, and, as they collide,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502880,
    "dDurationMs": 5279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "create rippling vibrations that pass through\neach other and interfere, just like the ripples"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508159,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a pond. But as these waves get all mixed\nup and pulled back into the growing gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514029,
    "dDurationMs": 4551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well, they create interference patterns\nof increasing complexity as the waves overlapped."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518580,
    "dDurationMs": 5129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What we’re left with is a messy, bubbling\ninterference pattern of gravitationally bound"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523709,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter waves sloshing around in the shape\nof a halo."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527649,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That doesn’t sound anything like a WIMP\ndark matter halo, but it turns out that this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532089,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slosh of axion superfluid will have the same\ndensity distribution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536709,
    "dDurationMs": 4951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s dense towards the center of the halo,\nand then drops in density as you go out in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541660,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same way as regular cold dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544340,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means it potentially has exactly the\nsame gravitational field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547520,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s especially true if your axion type\nis of the more standard breed in which the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552600,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass is around 10^-5 eV."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556610,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The typical size of the bubbly structure in\naxion dark matter is of order the de Broglie"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562790,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavelength, which is a few hundred meters\nor less for more standard types of axion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569110,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the scale of even a solar system, this\ntype of axionic dark matter would look very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574020,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smooth, and its gravitational effect would\nbe pretty much indistinguishable from WIMP"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579160,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580160,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This isn’t a coincidence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582640,
    "dDurationMs": 6820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The large-scale average behavior of wavy axion\ndynamics is mathematically identical to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589460,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "large-scale average behavior of WIMP dynamics\nbecause both of these systems can broadly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595580,
    "dDurationMs": 5879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be described as cold, collisionless fluids\nwith no significant interactions apart from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601459,
    "dDurationMs": 1011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602470,
    "dDurationMs": 6239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if WIMP and axion dark matter look the\nsame, and WIMPs do a good job of explaining"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608709,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the evolution of structure in the universe,\nthen axionic dark matter is also a viable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613779,
    "dDurationMs": 1291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615070,
    "dDurationMs": 3130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why choose axions over WIMPs?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618200,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing it’s good to explore all the\npossible alternative explanations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622330,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And maybe this explains why no WIMP has ever\nbeen detected—it would be because they don’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628430,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629430,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, the fact that axionic dark\nmatter can very closely resemble WIMP dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634620,
    "dDurationMs": 3659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter makes it difficult to discriminate\nbetween the two."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638279,
    "dDurationMs": 4832,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s another reason to consider axionic\ndark matter over WIMPs—and that’s because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643111,
    "dDurationMs": 5529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it may do an even better job at explaining\nthe structures we see in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648640,
    "dDurationMs": 5389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While cold dark matter models like WIMPs predict\nthe large scale structure and the number of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654029,
    "dDurationMs": 6441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big galaxies in the universe very well, historically\nthey’ve struggled to reproduce the finer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660470,
    "dDurationMs": 2809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "details of structure formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663279,
    "dDurationMs": 6011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, CDM models predict that galaxies\nshould have very high densities at their centers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669290,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They also predict that there should be lots\nof smaller clumps of matter like dwarf galaxies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673760,
    "dDurationMs": 2310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and satellite galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676070,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CDM models make these predictions precisely\nbecause the dark matter in CDM models is cold—which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683270,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means its particles have lower speeds and\ntend to fall towards the center of their gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688290,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wells."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689910,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In most of our simulations, CDM models give\nyou a lot of small galaxies and give you large"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695360,
    "dDurationMs": 3169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies with a high concentration of stuff\nin the center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698529,
    "dDurationMs": 7651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in the real universe we don’t see nearly\nas many small structures as seen in most simulations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706180,
    "dDurationMs": 6839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also we see that in real large galaxies, density increases towards the center but then flattens"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713019,
    "dDurationMs": 3281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out, rather than producing a steep central\ncusp."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716300,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now I will say that newer simulations have\nbeen able to explain some of these discrepancies—for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721600,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example, by better simulating the role of\n“regular” matter in structure formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727070,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But another potential fix is to use axionic\ndark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732610,
    "dDurationMs": 6789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The axions that we’ve been talking about\nare very light at 10^-5-ish eV, but if we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739399,
    "dDurationMs": 7781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could tune that mass down even more—to a\nridiculous 10^-20 eV or so—then the structures"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747180,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produced by this dark matter would start to\nlook different to regular cold dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752140,
    "dDurationMs": 6309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A dark matter axion with such a low mass has\nan enormous de Broglie wavelength—perhaps"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758449,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thousands of light years long."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761029,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that case, the smallest structures such\ndark matter could form would also be thousands"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766399,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of light years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767399,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would be harder to make small galaxies,\nand it would be harder for this dark matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772389,
    "dDurationMs": 4871,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to clump up in the centers of large galaxies—making\nthe model more consistent with what we observe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777260,
    "dDurationMs": 2170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in our actual universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779430,
    "dDurationMs": 4779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Axionic dark matter with such extremely low\nmasses gives us so-called Fuzzy Dark Matter,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784209,
    "dDurationMs": 4531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a wavey, fuzzy nature apparent on astronomical\nscales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788740,
    "dDurationMs": 4401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This sort of dark matter wouldn’t just help\nwith our structure formation problems, it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793141,
    "dDurationMs": 5799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would actually be detectable because it should\nlead to a grainy structure from galactic-scale"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798940,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interference patterns."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801060,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are proposals for detecting this, for\nexample by looking for its effect in gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805320,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lensing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806320,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s nothing conclusive yet, but upcoming\ngiant surveys have a hope of spotting fuzzy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811320,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter if its real."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813380,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, there’s a semi-reasonable motivation\nfor proposing such extremely light axions—they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818880,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are actually predicted by string theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821980,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So now we just need string theory to be right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824200,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like I said, it’s not clear that the cold\ndark matter model actually fails, but to dig"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829860,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the potential issues with the glorious\nLambda-CDM cosmological model we’re going"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834780,
    "dDurationMs": 1490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to need another episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836270,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which we’ll get to soon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837270,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the meantime, rest assured that dark matter\nwill keep doing its good work, whether as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843390,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "WIMPs or overlapping oscillations of an invisible\nsuperfluid that stitches together our galactic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849270,
    "dDurationMs": 830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
